WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.160
What do galaxies, cloud formations,
your nervous system,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.560
mountain ranges and coastlines
all have in common?

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.360
They all contain never ending
patterns known as fractals.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.720
A classic example of a fractal
in nature is broccoli -

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.840
in that the whole stalk is a similar
version of one of its branches.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:19.680
So cut off one piece

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.160
and you're left with a smaller
version of the entire broccoli.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.160
Snowflakes are another example.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.400
It's often said that no two
snowflakes are ever the same

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.840
and fractals offer
a fascinating explanation

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.720
as to why nature works in this way -

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.640
why nature continuously
creates new, self-replicating

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.240
yet unique structures and how
the smallest things in existence

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.720
are necessary components
of the greater whole.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.240
The term fractal was coined
by Benoit Mandelbrot

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.840
who was working at
computer giant IBM in 1980.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.000
Mandlebrot had been fascinated
by discoveries of mathematicians

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.600
from the early 19th Century

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.200
who were attempting to define their
understanding of what a curve is.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.200
Experiments such as
Georg Cantor's discovery

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.480
that a single line
could be divided forever

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.000
and Helge von Koch's triangle -

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.760
a shape that has an infinite
perimeter but a finite area -

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.320
resulted in the term 'monsters'.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.560
Mandelbrot used the modern computing
powers developed by IBM

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.720
to run these monster equations
millions of times over.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.720
This process led him
to a breakthrough equation

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.280
combining the patterns
found in previous monsters

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.400
resulting in his own set of numbers.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.200
This would become known
as the Mandelbrot set -

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.000
an infinite geometrical
visualisation of a fractal.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.880
One of the most amazing things
about the Mandelbrot set

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.880
is that theoretically,
if left by itself,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.640
would continue to create
infinitely new patterns

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.000
from the original structure

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.840
proving that something
could be magnified forever.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.800
Fractal geometry is currently
applied in many fields.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.080
For example,
research into climate change

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.640
and the trajectory
of dangerous meteorites,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.120
helping with cancer research

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.760
by helping to identify
the growth of mutated cells.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.320
It's even believed by some that the
universe itself may be a fractal

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.400
and as you zoomed in

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.120
you would discover it's made up
of billions of galaxies.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.880
Inside of those galaxies,
you would find trillions of stars

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.400
and billions of solar systems
and planets.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.320
And on one of those planets
you would find Earth.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.920
On Earth you would find continents,
cities and a human.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.440
And inside of that human
you would find a brain

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.360
made of millions of cells

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.440
in which you would find
trillions of synapses firing away.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.520
And inside of those
you would find DNA

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.840
Inside DNA you would find atoms,
electrons, protons, neutrons.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.600
Deeper still you would find quarks,
neutrinos and so on

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.000
and then, just maybe,
continuously deeper into infinity.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.000
Some believe that, due to their
highly complex and mysterious nature,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.520
the greatest use of fractals
is yet to be discovered.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.000
Thanks for watching.

